# Code Review Report — TASK-002: Implement Domain Entities

**Reviewer:** Principal Engineer
**Date:** 2025-07-14
**Scope:** `src/DataViewer.Domain/Entities/` (6 files) + supporting enums and project configuration

---

## 1. Summary

> **Verdict: ✅ APPROVE WITH MINOR REQUESTS**

All six entities are present and satisfy every field listed in the acceptance criteria. The project file correctly enforces zero external NuGet dependencies, nullable reference types are enabled both at the project level and per-file, and the XML documentation is exemplary. A handful of lower-severity issues need to be addressed before the layer is consumed by upper layers; none are blockers, but two are MUST FIX because they will produce subtle runtime bugs and architectural drift.

---

## 2. Correctness

Every acceptance-criterion field has been verified against each entity.

| Entity | AC Fields | Status | Gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| `User` | Id, UserName, Email, PasswordHash, Role(enum), IsLocked, LockoutUntil, FailedLoginCount, CreatedAt, LastLoginAt | ✅ All present | — |
| `RefreshToken` | Id, UserId(FK), TokenHash, ExpiresAt, IsRevoked, CreatedAt, RevokedAt, nav to User | ✅ All present | — |
| `CredentialProfile` | Id, Name, AccessKeyId, EncryptedSecretKey(byte[]), Region, BucketName, KeyPrefix(?), IsActive, IsDeleted, CreatedAt, UpdatedAt, CreatedByUserId | ✅ All present | — |
| `AuditLogEntry` | Id, UserId, ActionType(enum), TimestampUtc, IpAddress, Parameters(?), ResultCount(?), S3ObjectKey(?), ProfileName(?) | ✅ All present | — |
| `UserPreference` | UserId(PK/FK), DefaultPageSize, DefaultDateRangeDays, PreferredProfileId(?) | ✅ All present | — |
| `SystemSettings` | Id=1(int), JwtAccessTokenMinutes, JwtRefreshTokenHours, BodySizeCapMb, LockoutThreshold, UpdatedAt | ✅ All present | — |

### Functional gaps / behavioural concerns

1. **`User.cs` line 58 — `CreatedAt` default:** `= DateTime.UtcNow` is evaluated **at object construction time**, not at database persist time. When EF Core materialises an existing row from the database, the column value will correctly be loaded from the DB, so this is safe at runtime — but it means two `new User()` instances created in the same test will have `CreatedAt` values that differ by microseconds, making equality testing fragile. The same applies to `RefreshToken.CreatedAt` (line 40), `CredentialProfile.CreatedAt` / `UpdatedAt` (lines 68, 71), `AuditLogEntry.TimestampUtc` (line 44), and `SystemSettings.UpdatedAt` (line 60). The convention is acceptable if the Infrastructure layer always overwrites these with `DateTime.UtcNow` captured at command-handling time, but that contract is not written down anywhere (see Action Item #1).

2. **`SystemSettings.cs` — singleton enforcement is advisory only.** The comment explains that Id = 1 is always the singleton sentinel, but there is no constructor, factory method, or private setter preventing `new SystemSettings { Id = 42 }`. The constraint is only enforced in prose and in the seed migration. A rogue application layer call could insert a second row undetected (see Action Item #2).

3. **`CredentialProfile.cs` — `UpdatedAt` is not automatically maintained.** There is no `OnUpdating` hook or domain event to bump `UpdatedAt` when a profile is mutated. Callers must remember to set it manually, which is error-prone (see Action Item #3).

4. **`DataViewer.Core` entity conflict.** The solution still contains `src/DataViewer.Core/Domain/User.cs`, `RefreshToken.cs`, `UserPreference.cs`, `AuditLog.cs`, and `S3CredentialProfile.cs`. These are the **old** domain models (using `int` PKs, string roles, `DefaultDateRangeHours` instead of `DefaultDateRangeDays`, `EncryptedSecretAccessKey:string` instead of `byte[]`, etc.) and are now **in direct conflict** with the new TASK-002 entities. The solution will not have a coherent domain model until the `DataViewer.Core` models are removed or migrated (see Action Item #4 — MUST FIX).

---

## 3. Code Quality

### Positives
- XML documentation is thorough and consistent across all six files — every public member has a `<summary>` and most have a `<remarks>`.
- Null safety is properly annotated: non-nullable strings default to `string.Empty`; required navigation properties use the `null!` suppressor pattern with an explanatory comment.
- Namespace-scoped `using` directives (C# 10 file-scoped namespaces) are used consistently.
- `ICollection<T>` is correctly used for the `User.RefreshTokens` collection — programming to an interface rather than a concrete type.

### Issues

**[Readability] `User.cs` line 71 — inconsistent collection initialisation syntax.**
```csharp
// User.cs line 71 — old initialiser syntax
public ICollection<RefreshToken> RefreshTokens { get; set; } = new List<RefreshToken>();
```
Everywhere else in the solution (e.g., `DataViewer.Core/Domain/User.cs`) the C# 12 collection expression `= []` is used. This is purely stylistic, but inconsistency will cause friction in code review going forward. Prefer `= []` to match the target language version (LangVersion 12 is already set in the csproj).

**[Naming] `AuditLogEntry.cs` — entity named `AuditLogEntry`, but the corresponding `DataViewer.Core` entity is `AuditLog`.** The mismatch (singular entry vs. log) should be deliberately reconciled in the migration plan from Core → Domain, not left to diverge silently (also covered under Action Item #4).

**[SOLID / SRP] `UserPreference.cs` line 29 — default values baked into the entity.**
```csharp
public int DefaultPageSize { get; set; } = 25;
public int DefaultDateRangeDays { get; set; } = 7;
```
Default values for UI preferences are a **policy decision** that ideally lives in the Application layer (or pulled from `SystemSettings`), not hardcoded in the Domain entity. If the product owner changes the default page size, it requires a Domain model change rather than an application configuration change. Consider removing defaults here and having the Application layer apply them on first-use materialisation (see Action Item #5 — NICE TO HAVE).

**[SOLID / OCP] `SystemSettings.cs` — no range guards on any numeric property.** `LockoutThreshold = 0` disables lockout (documented), but `JwtAccessTokenMinutes = 0` or `BodySizeCapMb = -1` have no documented meaning and could cause undefined behaviour downstream. The Domain layer is the right place to own these invariants via a private setter or a `Validate()` method (see Action Item #6 — NICE TO HAVE).

---

## 4. Security

**[HIGH] `CredentialProfile.cs` line 34 — `AccessKeyId` stored in plain text is correctly documented, but there is no indication of its expected format or length.**
AWS Access Key IDs are always 20 uppercase alphanumeric characters starting with `AKIA` (or similar prefix for temporary credentials). Without a maximum-length annotation or domain validation, a caller could persist an arbitrarily long string into this field. While format validation belongs in the Application layer, the Domain entity should signal intent (see Action Item #7).

**[MEDIUM] `AuditLogEntry.cs` line 53 — `IpAddress` is a plain `string` with no validation.**
The field's XML doc notes it is extracted from `X-Forwarded-For`. That header is **trivially forgeable** by any client and can contain multiple comma-separated addresses. If the Application layer forwards the raw header value into this field, it becomes a stored, unvalidated string that could exceed database column widths or contain misleading data. This is not a Domain-layer fix per se, but the Domain entity should document the expectation that the Infrastructure/API layer is responsible for normalising and validating this value before it reaches the entity (see Action Item #8).

**[LOW] `User.cs` lines 19-25 — `UserName` and `Email` have no maximum-length signals.**
Without a `[MaxLength]` attribute (not available in a pure Domain layer) or a domain constant, EF Core will default to `nvarchar(max)` unless the Infrastructure mapping adds column constraints. Consider declaring public constants `MaxUserNameLength = 64` and `MaxEmailLength = 254` (the RFC 5321 limit) on the entity class to communicate intent to the Infrastructure mapping layer without taking a dependency on `System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations` (see Action Item #9).

**[LOW] `RefreshToken.cs` line 29 — `TokenHash` field comment says SHA-256 but that is an application-layer concern.**
The entity correctly stores only the hash, but there is no domain constant or marker indicating the expected byte length of the hash (32 bytes = 64 hex chars). This makes it impossible for the Infrastructure layer to set an appropriate column size without reading the application code (see Action Item #9).

**[INFO] No secrets are exposed** in the domain entities themselves. The `EncryptedSecretKey` is correctly typed as `byte[]` and documented as AES-256 encrypted. `PasswordHash` is documented as bcrypt. These are correct.

---

## 5. Test Coverage

> **Status: ❌ No test suite exists for the Domain layer.**

The `DataViewer.Tests` project is registered in `DataViewer.sln` but the `tests/` directory does not exist on disk. There are zero test files anywhere in the repository (confirmed by a recursive search for `[Fact]`, `[Theory]`, `[Test]`, `[TestClass]`).

For entities this simple (plain data containers), full integration tests are not warranted, but the following unit tests MUST be written before the layer is depended on by Application:

| # | Test Case | Why |
|---|---|---|
| T-01 | `new User()` → `Id != Guid.Empty`, `Role == UserRole.Viewer`, `IsLocked == false`, `FailedLoginCount == 0` | Guards against accidental default-value regressions |
| T-02 | `new RefreshToken()` → `IsRevoked == false`, `Id != Guid.Empty`, `User` collection property safe to traverse | Guards nav-property initialisation |
| T-03 | `new SystemSettings()` → `Id == 1`, all int fields > 0 | Documents and enforces singleton sentinel and safe defaults |
| T-04 | `new UserPreference()` → `DefaultPageSize == 25`, `DefaultDateRangeDays == 7`, `PreferredProfileId == null` | Documents defaults |
| T-05 | `new CredentialProfile()` → `IsActive == false`, `IsDeleted == false`, `EncryptedSecretKey` is empty array not null | Guards against null-reference exceptions downstream |
| T-06 | `new AuditLogEntry()` → `TimestampUtc` is close to `DateTime.UtcNow` (within 1 second) | Guards clock-capture behaviour |
| T-07 | `UserRole` enum — verify `Viewer = 0`, `Admin = 1` (protect DB persistence ordinals) | Regression guard against enum reordering |
| T-08 | `AuditActionType` enum — verify all 11 members and their explicit int values | Same as above |
| T-09 | Two `new User()` instances → `Id` values are not equal | Guid.NewGuid() uniqueness sanity check |
| T-10 | `new SystemSettings { Id = 99 }` compiles but violates intent — documents need for factory method | Documents Action Item #2 |

---

## 6. Performance

**[LOW] `User.cs` line 71 — `new List<RefreshToken>()` allocates on every entity instantiation**, including when EF Core materialises the entity from a database query where lazy or explicit loading is used instead of `Include()`. The allocation is tiny, but it means every `SELECT * FROM Users` row materialises an empty `List<T>`. The C# 12 collection expression `= []` compiles to the same allocation; the real fix is to understand that EF Core will replace this collection reference when it materialises a tracked entity anyway. No action needed, but the inconsistency with `= []` elsewhere should still be fixed.

**[MEDIUM] `CreatedAt` / `UpdatedAt` set via `DateTime.UtcNow` in property initialisers.** This is evaluated at `new` time, not at EF Core materialisation time (EF Core bypasses constructors and property initialisers during hydration). This means there is no performance or correctness issue for reads. However, it does mean that if an entity is constructed in a factory and then not immediately saved (e.g., sitting in a queue), the timestamp will drift. This should be documented as a constraint or mitigated by setting the timestamp in the repository/unit-of-work (see Action Item #1).

**[INFO] No N+1 risks in the Domain layer itself** — navigation properties are present for EF Core to `Include()` correctly but no lazy-loading proxies are configured at this layer.

---

## 7. Action Items

### 🔴 MUST FIX

**1. Remove or reconcile the conflicting `DataViewer.Core` domain models.**
`src/DataViewer.Core/Domain/` contains `User.cs`, `RefreshToken.cs`, `UserPreference.cs`, `AuditLog.cs`, and `S3CredentialProfile.cs` — all using `int` PKs, `string` roles, and different field names. Leaving both sets of models in the same solution will cause EF Core migration ambiguity, namespace confusion, and potentially two separate DbContexts mapping the same database tables. Either:
- Delete the Core domain models and update all references to point to `DataViewer.Domain.Entities`, **or**
- Introduce a clear mapping layer and document which model is the authoritative domain model.
This is the single highest-priority action item.

**2. Create the `tests/DataViewer.Tests` project directory and implement the test suite.**
The project is registered in the `.sln` but the directory is missing. The build will fail on a clean clone, and there is no confidence that the entity defaults or enum ordinals are regression-proof. Implement at minimum the ten test cases listed in Section 5.

**3. Protect the `SystemSettings` singleton Id.**
`src/DataViewer.Domain/Entities/SystemSettings.cs` line 31:
```csharp
// Current — fully public setter, easily violated
public int Id { get; set; } = 1;
```
Change to a `private set` (or `init`) with a factory method or constructor to prevent accidental multi-row inserts:
```csharp
// Option A — init-only (C# 9+)
public int Id { get; init; } = 1;

// Option B — private setter with static factory
private SystemSettings() { }
public static SystemSettings CreateSingleton() => new() { Id = 1 };
```

### 🟡 NICE TO HAVE

**4. Document the `CreatedAt`/`UpdatedAt` timestamp ownership contract.**
Add an XML `<remarks>` to each timestamp property (or a single file-level remark per entity) stating: *"This value is set by the Application/Infrastructure layer at command-handling time. The constructor default is a safety net only and should not be relied upon for persistence semantics."* This prevents the subtle drift bug described in Section 6.

**5. Fix collection initialisation inconsistency.**
`src/DataViewer.Domain/Entities/User.cs` line 71:
```csharp
// Before
public ICollection<RefreshToken> RefreshTokens { get; set; } = new List<RefreshToken>();

// After — consistent with LangVersion 12 used elsewhere in the solution
public ICollection<RefreshToken> RefreshTokens { get; set; } = [];
```

**6. Add domain-level string-length constants to `User`, `CredentialProfile`, and `RefreshToken`.**
Without a dependency on `DataAnnotations`, declare public constants on each entity class:
```csharp
// User.cs
public const int MaxUserNameLength = 64;
public const int MaxEmailLength = 254;  // RFC 5321

// CredentialProfile.cs
public const int AccessKeyIdLength = 20;  // AWS IAM fixed length

// RefreshToken.cs
public const int TokenHashLength = 64;    // SHA-256 hex = 64 chars
```
These are consumed by the Infrastructure mapping layer to set `HasMaxLength()` and by validators without creating a cross-layer dependency.

**7. Remove `DefaultPageSize` and `DefaultDateRangeDays` defaults from `UserPreference` entity.**
Move the defaults to an `ApplicationDefaults` static class in the Application layer (or drive them from `SystemSettings`). The Domain entity should carry `0` as the zero-value and let the Application layer apply policy defaults on read. This prevents a Domain change being required when a product decision changes the default page size.

**8. Add a `LockoutDurationMinutes` field to `SystemSettings`.**
Currently the lockout *threshold* is configurable, but the lockout *duration* (how many minutes until `LockoutUntil` expires) is presumably hardcoded in the Application layer. If so, this is an inconsistency — one half of the lockout policy is in `SystemSettings`, the other half is a magic number. Add `LockoutDurationMinutes` to `SystemSettings` for full configurability.

**9. Add inline guidance on `IpAddress` normalisation.**
`src/DataViewer.Domain/Entities/AuditLogEntry.cs` line 53 — Extend the XML `<summary>` to explicitly state that the Application/API layer MUST normalise `X-Forwarded-For` to a single validated IP string before assigning it to this field, protecting against header injection storing multi-value or malformed strings in the audit log.


